# Appendix D — Browser programs

Literate uses small browser programs for interactive screen behavior and an isolated
browser worker for publication assets. None of these programs defines fragment
semantics. They consume escaped containers or bounded models prepared by the engine
and screen renderers.

The conceptual routes are [Previewing the explanation](previewing_the_explanation.html),
[Exploring the fragment graph](exploring_fragment_graph.html), and
[Portable publication resources](portable_publication_resources.html). Mechanical
entry-point assembly is recorded in
[Appendix A — Program assembly maps](assembly.html).

## Program inventory

### Markdown Mermaid preview

[Browser Mermaid preview](mermaid_preview.html) owns the readable browser entry source
for the Markdown preview renderer. The build bundles it as a local preview script,
and the manifest loads that bundle through `markdown.previewScripts`.

Its input is the DOM, not an editor command channel:

- plain Mermaid fences are emitted as escaped `pre.literate-mermaid` containers;
- the nearest `.literate-document` wrapper supplies the catalogue theme through
  `data-literate-theme`;
- source text comes from the container's `textContent`;
- successful strict SVG replaces the container's inner markup;
- current failures replace it with text and an alert role, preserving the source.

A named Literate contribution with Mermaid as its programming language is not a
preview diagram and never receives this container shape.

### Inline fragment exploration

[Optional fragment exploration](publication-exploration.html) owns one browser
initializer used in two hosts. Generated HTML embeds the self-contained initializer.
Markdown preview loads the locally bundled preview script declared in the manifest.
Both call `initializePublicationExploration(document)` and operate on the same inert
model and DOM attributes.

The client enhances existing semantic links with disclosure buttons. It creates a
panel only after validating and preflighting the complete requested family. Ordinary
definition links exist independently and remain usable when scripting, payload
validation, or panel allocation is unavailable.

### Publication Mermaid browser and worker

[Isolated vector diagrams](publication-mermaid.html) is the Node-side controller for
static publication diagrams. [A browser that produces assets](publication-mermaid-worker.html)
owns the child process that launches and connects to an explicitly selected Chromium.
A separate offline browser bundle installs Mermaid and supported integrations into
the isolated page.

This path produces vector PDF and SVG companions for print publication. It is not used
for ordinary generation or editor preview. Books without Mermaid diagrams do not
need the publication browser path.

## Markdown preview lifecycle

The Mermaid preview initializes its local renderer immediately with automatic generic
Mermaid scanning disabled. Startup waits for `DOMContentLoaded` only when the body is
not yet available. It then observes:

- `vscode.markdown.updateContent` on `window`, which signals preview replacement or
  refresh;
- changes to `data-literate-theme` beneath the body;
- the initial document state.

Updates are coalesced into one serial drain. Each diagram records state in a `WeakMap`
keyed by its actual container. A render attempt captures the current container markup,
source, and theme. Its result is installed only if the same container is connected,
still matches the selector, still contains that markup, and still selects that theme.
Stale asynchronous work is discarded without becoming an error.

Renderer initialization and rendering are serialized because configuration is shared
browser state. Interactive callback binding is deliberately omitted.

The exploration client similarly initializes once per document. It scans immediately,
on `DOMContentLoaded`, on `vscode.markdown.updateContent`, and after relevant DOM
mutations. Reconciliation destroys panels whose root, source use, target, payload, or
link identity changed. Private maps—not editable DOM attributes—own controls, panels,
focus restoration, and resource charges.

## Browser data and event contracts

### Mermaid DOM contract

A preview diagram container has the selector `pre.literate-mermaid`. Its text is the
complete escaped Mermaid source. The project wrapper's `data-literate-theme` names a
trusted catalogue entry. Browser code does not read arbitrary per-diagram
configuration from attributes or source.

The only host event used by this client is `vscode.markdown.updateContent`. No source
text is sent through `postMessage`, and the browser program has no VS Code API object.

### Exploration payload format

Each `.literate-document` may contain one inert script element:

```text
script.literate-exploration-data[type="application/json"]
```

Its JSON object has protocol `version: 1` and these members:

- `parts`: an object from stable contribution IDs to ordered segment arrays;
- `families`: an object from stable family targets to a display name, ordered
  contribution IDs, and an optional validated destination;
- `unavailable`: an optional reason indicating that the host refused to serialize a
  usable payload.

A segment always carries `text`. A reference segment additionally carries
`reference: true` and may carry a resolved `target`. The serializer escapes
script-sensitive characters so the JSON remains inert.

The browser validates shape, protocol version, identifiers, strings, arrays, links,
and complete resource cost before using the model. It creates source text with text
nodes, never by interpreting segment text as markup.

Baseline anchors and source uses carry logical identity through
`data-literate-target` and `data-literate-explore`. Disclosure controls and panels are
browser-owned enhancements; removing the script leaves ordinary links intact.

### Navigation destinations

The exploration browser accepts only destinations justified by the host context:

- a matching fragment in the current document;
- a validated `./` HTML chapter path ending in the matching fragment;
- a narrowly shaped native Literate preview capability;
- an HTTPS destination only when an existing same-target baseline link supplied it.

JSON alone cannot authorize an HTTPS destination or arbitrary editor command. Native
preview capabilities remain opaque; the editor extension validates their current
session, trust, workspace, expiry, and semantic target when activated. See
[Previewing the explanation](previewing_the_explanation.html).

### Publication worker request

The publication controller creates a private directory and writes `request.json` with
this bounded shape:

```json
{
  "sources": ["complete Mermaid source"],
  "theme": "catalogue-theme-name",
  "fontSize": 16
}
```

The worker receives the private directory and explicit browser executable as process
arguments. It validates the request again, including the bounded CSS-pixel font size,
renders each unique source in order, and
writes numeric scratch artifacts as `<index>.pdf` and `<index>.svg`. Binary data never
travels through standard output. Standard output and error are treated as bounded
worker diagnostics, not a result protocol.

The parent validates every artifact's regular-file status, size, and file signature
before returning bytes to publication. Scratch names never come from source paths.
The complete temporary directory, including browser profile, is removed on every
completion path.

## Preview Mermaid policy

The Markdown preview owns fixed configuration:

- strict Mermaid security;
- no automatic scan of generic `.mermaid` elements;
- no callback installation;
- no authored directives or YAML frontmatter;
- no HTML labels;
- a maximum of 50,000 source characters and 500 edges;
- a theme selected only from Literate's trusted local catalogue.

The renderer, theme configuration, and integrations are bundled locally; the
document cannot select a different Mermaid runtime. Unlike static publication, live
preview does not add its own request interception layer, so it relies on the Markdown
preview host's resource policy for any URL-like content Mermaid might interpret.
Syntax or policy failures are shown as text beside the original source and do not stop
other diagrams.

## Exploration resource limits

The exploration protocol and client enforce the same fixed limits:

- serialized payload: 1 MiB in UTF-8;
- visible nesting: eight layers;
- live conservatively accounted panel content: 256 KiB per document;
- live panels: 200;
- contributions visited for one panel: 200;
- segments visited for one panel: 4096.

Payload overflow produces a small unavailable model. Panel overflow, a cycle, an
excessive path, duplicate contribution identity, invalid link, or malformed segment
refuses the whole requested panel. Code is never silently truncated. Collapsing or
retiring a panel recursively releases its descendants and private resource charge.

## Publication browser isolation

Static publication rendering applies stricter policy than the live preview because it
produces portable artifacts:

- Mermaid directives, frontmatter, authored HTML, authored CSS commands, links, and
  interactions are rejected before browser launch;
- each diagram receives a fresh browser context;
- one fixed in-memory bootstrap document is allowed, while CSP and request
  interception reject network, file, image, font, frame, form, popup, and subsequent
  navigation activity;
- the trusted renderer bundle is injected as code, but source is passed as structured
  evaluation data rather than interpolated into HTML or JavaScript;
- the returned SVG is checked for active elements, event handlers, external
  references, unsafe CSS URLs, invalid geometry, and oversized dimensions before PDF
  printing;
- the browser sandbox remains enabled; no weakened fallback is attempted.

The controller bounds unique diagram count, source length, per-format artifact size,
aggregate artifact bytes, worker lifetime, and worker logs. The worker independently
bounds page timeouts, browser launch diagnostics, geometry, and each batch result.
Any failure rejects publication before the final output transaction.

## Browser selection and ownership

Publication never discovers or downloads a browser. The author or user selects an
absolute executable path through the CLI or restricted editor setting. The controller
checks that the executable is an accessible regular file before spawning a separate
worker process without a shell.

The controller owns the worker deadline and process group. The worker owns the
Chromium child, private profile, fresh browser contexts, and cooperative close. A
hung or failed browser cannot become a successful partial publication merely because
one artifact was already written to the private directory.

The exact external runtime prerequisites are maintained in package and contributor
metadata. The stable contract here is explicit selection, sandbox-capable Chromium,
no automatic installation, private temporary state, and fail-closed artifact
validation.

## Failure lookup

- **A plain Mermaid fence remains source:** confirm the preview script is packaged and
  that the fence info is exactly `mermaid`; named fragment fences intentionally remain
  code.
- **A diagram shows an alert:** inspect the preserved source for syntax, directives,
  frontmatter, size, or edge-limit failures.
- **A diagram flashes an old result:** current-state checks should reject it; trace the
  container markup, selected theme, and serial render queue in
  [Browser Mermaid preview](mermaid_preview.html).
- **No Explore button appears:** verify an accepted resolved reference, a valid
  versioned payload, and a live root outside an owned panel.
- **A panel refuses to open:** the status text distinguishes cycles, depth, panel
  capacity, unavailable families, and DOM/contribution budgets; the definition link
  remains the fallback.
- **A preview link is inert:** destination authority may still be pending, expired,
  conflicting, untrusted, or invalid for the current workspace.
- **Publication diagrams fail before output:** inspect source policy, explicit browser
  selection, worker diagnostics, blocked-resource reports, vector validation, and
  artifact limits. Previous published output is preserved.
